<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sultan al-Atrash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1891–1982</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Pasha al-Atrash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Atrash was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> leader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He fought in the Arab Revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and in 1925 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a> against the French Mandate, which spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> before it was crushed in 1927. He became a national hero of Syria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash.jpg" alt="Sultan al-Atrash" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Colony (Jerusalem) Photography Dept. (photographer unnamed) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Pasha_Al-Atrash2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
